    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i just got my blood work back from my doctor and the ldl number is higher than it was last year. can u explain what ldl and hdl actually do in the body and why the ldl one is the one they worry about more?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein) and HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein) are types of lipoproteins, which are proteins that carry fats (cholesterol) in the bloodstream. HDL helps remove excess cholesterol from the bloodstream and transport it to the liver for disposal. On the other hand, LDL is responsible for delivering cholesterol to the body's cells and tissues. LDL is often referred to as the \"bad\" cholesterol because high levels can lead to plaque buildup in arteries, increasing the risk of heart disease.",
