    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get that. but like, what is actually happening inside the arteries when that plaque builds up? is it just sitting there or is it doing something specific to the walls of the artery?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When plaque builds up in the arteries, the deposits can cause inflammation, irritation, and scarring of the arterial walls. The LDL cholesterol in the plaque can also release free radicals that damage the inner lining of the artery, making it more susceptible to rupture. This can lead to the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque, which is a fluffy white mass of dead cells, cholesterol crystals, and fat that can cause the artery to narrow and harden. Over time, this can restrict blood flow to vital organs, such as the heart and brain.",
      "source": "target",
